@title Member Profile Database
@subtitle Choosing a database for the new member-profile service
@author Accounts team, Lattice Fitness
@date 2026-11-04

Decision due 2026-11-04.

kpi Profiles | 9 million | ~2 KB each
kpi Peak reads | 3,500/s
kpi Peak writes | 220/s

Access is mostly key lookups, plus a few admin searches by email and gym. The options are PostgreSQL (RDS), DynamoDB and MongoDB Atlas.

# Comparison
table = Option,Query flexibility,Scaling model,Ops effort,Multi-region; PostgreSQL,high,vertical plus read replicas,medium,read replicas only; DynamoDB,low,automatic,low,global tables; MongoDB Atlas,medium,sharding,medium,global clusters

# Cost at scale
data cost = traffic,PostgreSQL,DynamoDB,MongoDB Atlas; Today,780,540,910; 3x traffic,1640,1420,1870; 10x traffic,4300,4650,5200; 20x traffic,8100,9200,9900
chart bar cost unit=$ title="Estimated monthly cost"

# Pros and cons
## PostgreSQL (RDS)
- Pro: flexible queries for admin search.
- Pro: cheapest at 10x traffic.
- Con: scales vertically, with read replicas only for reads.
- Con: medium ops effort.

## DynamoDB
- Pro: automatic scaling and low ops effort.
- Pro: cheapest today and at 3x.
- Con: low query flexibility; admin search needs extra indexes.

## MongoDB Atlas
- Pro: global clusters for multi-region.
- Con: most expensive at every traffic level.
- Con: sharding adds ops effort.

# Recommendation
! Choose **PostgreSQL**. Admin search needs flexible queries, and its cost stays within $220 a month of DynamoDB at our realistic 3x horizon.

!! Write volume above 2,000 per second would force us to revisit sharding.
